@page "/projects/portfolio/overview"
@page "/projects/web-development/overview"

<div class="project-page page-content">
	<header class="project-header">
		<p class="eyebrow">Web Development</p>
		<h1>Overview</h1>
		<p class="tagline">A practical portfolio for showing shipped work, engineering judgment, and the systems behind the screen.</p>
	</header>

	<section class="project-section">
		<h2>What This Site Represents</h2>
		<p>
			This portfolio is a living .NET application rather than a static resume page. It highlights my background in cloud-native .NET, backend architecture, API design, data modeling, frontend implementation, and independent product work.
		</p>
		<p>
			The content is drawn from my professional experience across C#, ASP.NET Core, Blazor, Angular, SQL Server, Azure, CI/CD pipelines, and test-focused delivery. The surrounding codebase also includes a separate Bible API project that demonstrates Minimal APIs, Swagger documentation, HTTP client integration, Docker packaging, and Azure Container Apps deployment.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="project-section">
		<h2>Current Focus</h2>
		<div class="project-grid">
			<article>
				<h3>Backend Systems</h3>
				<p>Service-layer design, API boundaries, validation, configuration, external integrations, and maintainable domain logic.</p>
			</article>
			<article>
				<h3>Cloud Delivery</h3>
				<p>Azure-first deployment patterns, containerized services, environment-based configuration, and operational documentation.</p>
			</article>
			<article>
				<h3>Frontend Craft</h3>
				<p>Blazor and Angular experience, responsive layouts, accessible links, and UI that keeps technical content readable.</p>
			</article>
			<article>
				<h3>Independent Shipping</h3>
				<p>Published iOS game work in Unity and C#, self-published writing, and ongoing learning through Boot.dev and practice platforms.</p>
			</article>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="project-section">
		<h2>What I Want Visitors To See</h2>
		<ul class="project-list">
			<li>I can work across the stack without losing sight of maintainability.</li>
			<li>I am strongest in backend-heavy systems, especially .NET services and integration work.</li>
			<li>I document the operational path, not just the happy-path code.</li>
			<li>I ship personal projects and keep learning in public-adjacent spaces.</li>
		</ul>
	</section>
</div>
